# gem-publish/CHANGELOG

## 20261029

0.7.0: + -h and --help, and refuse any other switch, rather than taking either as a directory.

1. ~ bin/gem-publish: + usage(), + help?(), + unknown_switches(), + check_switches(), which prints the usage and exits 0 on -h or --help, or exits 1 on any other argument beginning with -. Each was taken as a directory, and refused as 'No such directory.' unless one by that name existed, which would have been built from and pushed.
2. ~ main(): calls check_switches() before check_clean().
3. + test/gem-publish_test.rb: -h and --help exit 0 and an unknown switch exits 1, each leaving the directory as it found it, and a directory named, or the current one, still reaches the cleanliness check.
4. ~ bin/gem-publish: /0.6.0/0.7.0/, and the date.
5. ~ README.md: + switches under usage, + running the tests.

## 20261028

0.6.0: Rewritten in Ruby, building each gem from the specification which was checked.

1. ~ bin/gem-publish: in Ruby rather than sh, the check having become a Ruby program held in a here-document.
2. ~ build_gem(): builds with Gem::Package.build from the Gem::Specification which check_clean() loaded, rather than `gem build` loading the gemspec again, so what is built is what was checked.
3. ~ build_gems(): a gem which fails to build stops the run before any push, and the gems already built are removed. The sh version pushed the rest, and pushed a literal *.gem from a directory which had built nothing.
4. ~ push_gems(), remove_gems(): push and remove only the gems the run built, rather than every *.gem in each directory.
5. ~ main(): exits 1 where any push fails.
6. ~ changes_in(): a directory which doesn't exist is refused with 'No such directory.'.
7. ~ bin/gem-publish: /0.5.1/0.6.0/, and the date.
8. ~ README.md: the description, and failed builds under usage.
9. ~ CHANGELOG: the heading names the project.

## 20261027

0.5.1: Refuse only where a file the gem is built from is uncommitted or untracked.

1. ~ bin/gem-publish: + built_from_status(), which loads each *.gemspec in the directory and runs `git status --porcelain` on its files, on the gemspec itself, and on every file within the repository which it reads or requires while loading. 0.5.0 checked the whole working tree, so an untracked file which no gemspec packages, notes kept beside the code, stopped switches.rb 0.14.1 from publishing.
2. Files read while loading count though they aren't packaged, since a version file shared across a monorepo, as Rails' RAILS_VERSION, changes the gem from outside its directory.
3. ~ check_clean(): refuses on what built_from_status() reports, rather than on `git status --porcelain` of the whole directory, and where the directory has no gemspec or one which doesn't load.
4. ~ bin/gem-publish: /0.5.0/0.5.1/, and the date.
5. ~ README.md: the check, in the description and under usage.
6. ~ TODO: 1, for what 0.5.1 checks.

## 20261022

0.5.0: Refuse to publish from a directory with uncommitted or untracked files.

1. ~ bin/gem-publish: + check_clean(), which stops the run, naming each directory and its files, where `git status --porcelain` reports anything or fails. `gem build` packages what is on disk, so an uncommitted edit or a stray file went out in a version which no commit or tag holds.
2. ~ bin/gem-publish: main() calls check_clean() on every directory before anything is built, and builds and pushes nothing where any fails it, so a sweep never publishes part of its list.
3. ~ bin/gem-publish: /0.4.0/0.5.0/, and the date.
4. ~ README.md: + the check, in the description and under usage.
5. + TODO: building from the tag, and the alternative rejected.

## 20260908

0.4.0: Take a list of directories, so one code covers a whole sweep.

1. ~ bin/gem-publish: build_gems() and push_gems() take the directories to work in, and main() defaults them to the current one. The OTP read at 0.3.0 was per directory, so it fired only where a directory held two gemspecs; eleven directories holding one apiece got no prompt at all and rubygems asked eleven times, a code apiece read from the authenticator minutes apart. The eleven are real: the LICENSE releases of 20260821 left that many gems tagged a patch ahead of what rubygems serves.
2. Everything is built before anything is pushed, and the ask sits between the two. Building is the slow half, so this puts the code's short life against the pushes alone rather than against the whole run.
3. ~ bin/gem-publish: + here, the directory returned to after each, so a relative name is read against where the command was run rather than against the last directory visited.
4. ~ bin/gem-publish: /0.3.0/0.4.0/, and the date.
5. ~ README.md: the description and the usage and multifactor sections all spoke of one directory.


## 20260828

0.3.0: Ask for the OTP code once and use it for every push.

1. ~ bin/gem-publish: + read_otp(), which asks for the multifactor code once, and push_gems(), which passes it to each `gem push` as `--otp`. With two or more gemspecs rubygems asked separately for each push, and a code entered for the first had usually expired by the time the second was wanted, so a two gemspec directory took two codes read from the authenticator a minute apart.
2. The ask happens only where more than one gem has been built, so the single gemspec case is untouched and rubygems does its own asking there.
3. A blank answer means no `--otp`, and rubygems asks per push as before: this is what someone without multifactor authentication wants, and the escape where the code has gone stale mid-run.
4. ~ bin/gem-publish: + build_gems(), main(), and the count of built gems the ask is conditioned on. The body was a flat sequence, which no longer holds a conditional and a prompt comfortably.
5. A code is good for thirty seconds either side of its window, so a directory of many gems can still outrun it; nothing here renews one mid-run.
6. ~ bin/gem-publish: /0.2.1/0.3.0/, and the date.
7. ~ README.md: the usage section named exactly one gemspec, which has been wrong since 0.2.0, and said nothing of the OTP prompt.


## 20260821

0.2.1: + LICENSE, which the README has named without one being present.

1. + LICENSE: the MIT text, copyright 2025-2026 thoran. The year is that of the first commit. The README has answered "MIT" under a heading of its own while the repository carried no licence text at all, so anyone taking it at its word had nothing to read and nothing to comply with.
2. ~ bin/gem-publish: /0.2.0/0.2.1/, and the date.


## 20260820

0.2.0: Build and push every gemspec in the directory.

1. ~ bin/gem-publish: `gem build *.gemspec` --> a loop over *.gemspec, and `gem push *.gem` --> a loop over *.gem. Each takes one file, so a glob hands it several and it uses the first or refuses outright. webdav carries two gemspecs, publishing the same code as webdav and as webdav.rb, and only ever built the one.
2. Where one of the two is already published, its push fails and the other still goes: rubygems refuses a repush, and nothing here stops on error.
3. ~ bin/gem-publish: /0.1.1/0.2.0/, and the date.


## 20260816

0.1.1: Populate .gitignore, which had been committed empty.

1. ~ .gitignore: the standard 47 lines. The file existed and was empty, which git-boot creates it as and nothing had since filled in.
2. ~ bin/gem-publish: /0.1.0/0.1.1/, and the date.


## 20260621

0.1.0: Ensure everything is world readable; + README.md, CHANGELOG.

1. ~ bin/gem-publish: + `chmod -R a+rX .`, so as to ensure all files are world readable before building the gem.
2. + README.md
3. + CHANGELOG


